---
import BaseLayout from '../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

const breadcrumbs = [
  { label: 'Home', url: '/en/' },
  { label: 'Blog', url: '/en/blog/' },
  { label: 'Senior Dog Care', url: '/en/blog/senior-dog-care/' }
];

const siteUrl = 'https://www.petaginghub.com';
const hrefLangAlternates = [
  { lang: 'zh-TW', url: siteUrl + '/blog/senior-dog-care' },
  { lang: 'en', url: siteUrl + '/en/blog/senior-dog-care' },
  { lang: 'x-default', url: siteUrl + '/blog/senior-dog-care' },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Senior Dog Care: Life Stage, Comfort, and Vet Questions"
  description="A practical, educational guide to senior dog care: life-stage context, changes to discuss with a veterinarian, home comfort, and gentle routines."
  lang="en"
  breadcrumbs={breadcrumbs}
  hrefLangAlternates={hrefLangAlternates}
>
  <article class="max-w-3xl mx-auto py-10 px-4">
    <header class="mb-8">
      <span class="inline-block bg-amber-100 text-amber-700 text-xs font-bold px-3 py-1 rounded-full mb-4">Dogs</span>
      <h1 class="text-4xl font-extrabold text-slate-900 mb-4">Senior Dog Care: A Practical Guide</h1>
      <p class="text-xl text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">A dog’s senior years are individual. Size, breed, expected lifespan, health, and daily life all shape the care conversations that matter most.</p>
    </header>

    <img
      src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1477884213360-7e9d7dcc1e48?w=1200&h=675&fit=crop&fm=webp"
      alt="An older dog resting outdoors"
      class="w-full aspect-video object-cover rounded-2xl mb-10"
      loading="eager"
    />

    <div class="space-y-10">
      <section>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900 mb-4">When Is a Dog Considered Senior?</h2>
        <p class="text-slate-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">There is no single age that makes every dog a senior. The American Animal Hospital Association describes the senior stage as the last part of a dog’s estimated lifespan, with timing shaped by breed and size. Use our <a href="/en/tools/dog-age-calculator/" class="text-blue-600 underline">Dog Age Calculator</a> as a size-aware educational reference—not as a diagnosis or a replacement for veterinary advice.</p>
        <p class="text-slate-700 leading-relaxed">A dog who seems well can still benefit from a proactive conversation about life stage. For broader context, see our <a href="/en/blog/dog-aging-by-breed/" class="text-blue-600 underline">dog aging by breed and size guide</a>.</p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900 mb-4">Changes Worth Discussing With a Veterinarian</h2>
        <div class="grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-4">
          <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 text-slate-700 text-sm">
            <li>Changes in activity, balance, or ease of getting up</li>
            <li>Changes in appetite, weight, thirst, or bathroom habits</li>
            <li>New sleep, hearing, vision, or behavior changes</li>
          </ul>
          <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 text-slate-700 text-sm">
            <li>Persistent coughing, discomfort, or reduced interest in usual activities</li>
            <li>New lumps, skin changes, or dental discomfort</li>
            <li>Any sudden or severe change from your dog’s normal behavior</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <p class="text-slate-700 text-sm leading-relaxed mt-4">These observations are not a diagnosis. They can help you and your veterinary team decide what, if anything, needs closer assessment. Our <a href="/en/blog/dog-aging-signs/" class="text-blue-600 underline">signs of aging in dogs guide</a> offers additional educational context.</p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900 mb-4">Support Comfort at Home</h2>
        <div class="bg-amber-50 rounded-2xl p-5 border border-amber-100">
          <ul class="space-y-3 text-slate-700 text-sm">
            <li><strong>Make movement easier:</strong> Add non-slip rugs, and consider steps or ramps for favorite resting places if your dog benefits from them.</li>
            <li><strong>Protect rest:</strong> Offer a comfortable bed in a quiet, easy-to-reach location.</li>
            <li><strong>Keep routines predictable:</strong> Gentle activity, familiar meals, and regular bathroom opportunities can support day-to-day comfort.</li>
            <li><strong>Observe rather than assume:</strong> Track meaningful changes and share them with a veterinarian instead of attributing every change to age.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900 mb-4">Questions for a Senior-Dog Wellness Visit</h2>
        <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 text-slate-700">
          <li>What life stage do you consider my dog to be in?</li>
          <li>Does my dog’s breed, size, or health history change the recommended preventive-care plan?</li>
          <li>Which changes in mobility, appetite, behavior, or bathroom habits should prompt a call?</li>
          <li>Are there practical home or activity adjustments that suit my individual dog?</li>
        </ul>
      </section>
    </div>

    <div class="mt-10 flex flex-wrap gap-4">
      <a href="/en/tools/dog-age-calculator/" class="px-5 py-3 bg-amber-500 hover:bg-amber-600 text-white font-semibold rounded-xl text-sm">Dog Age Calculator</a>
      <a href="/en/blog/senior-pet-health-guide/" class="px-5 py-3 bg-amber-100 hover:bg-amber-200 text-amber-800 font-semibold rounded-xl text-sm">Senior Pet Health Guide</a>
    </div>

    <p class="mt-10 text-xs text-slate-500 leading-relaxed"><strong>Educational note:</strong> This page provides general information only. It does not diagnose illness or replace an examination or advice from a veterinarian. Contact a veterinary professional promptly for sudden, severe, or concerning changes.</p>
  </article>

</BaseLayout>
